$DIR/main.p:6:1: //pu:stack: invalid size "2T" (want a number of bytes with an optional K, M or G suffix)
    Some directives take an argument, such as the symbol name in //pu:extern,
    and the rest take none. This directive was given text it does not take, or
    lacks the argument it needs, so the compiler would not understand it. To
    explain why a directive is there, write an ordinary comment on a line of its
    own.
    Go's //go: directives ignore text they do not expect. Plutonium directives
    change what the program means, so they are read exactly.
    Write the directive alone on its line:
        // slow path, kept out of line.
        //pu:noinline
        func slow() { ... }
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2603" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:3:1: //pu:stack applies only to func main in package main
    A directive belongs in the doc comment of the declaration it applies to: the
    lines of comment directly above it, with no blank line between. Each
    directive applies to one kind of declaration, such as functions or
    package-level variables, and //pu:build applies to a whole file and goes
    before the package clause. In any other place the compiler would not see it
    and it would do nothing.
    Go's //go: directives are ignored when misplaced. Plutonium directives
    change what the program means, so a directive that does nothing is an error.
    Put it directly above the declaration it applies to:
        //pu:noinline
        func slow() { ... }
    (see "Directives" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.3)
    (run "pluto explain E2602" for a longer explanation)
